      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "other"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Twin Arches, a 1,500‑acre natural area, features two sandstone arches that sit end‑to‑end in the Big South Fork region.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tn.gov/environment/program-areas/na-natural-areas/natural-areas-east-region/east-region-/na-na-twin-arches.html",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The arches form the largest natural bridge complex in Tennessee and rank among the world’s biggest.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tn.gov/environment/program-areas/na-natural-areas/natural-areas-east-region/east-region-/na-na-twin-arches.html",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The South Arch towers 103 feet high, while the North Arch reaches 62 feet, each offering dramatic spans of 135 and 93 feet respectively.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tn.gov/environment/program-areas/na-natural-areas/natural-areas-east-region/east-region-/na-na-twin-arches.html",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Rare plants such as Lucy Braun snakeroot and Cumberland sandwort grow on the arches’ sandstone, alongside a fragile moss‑lichen community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tn.gov/environment/program-areas/na-natural-areas/natural-areas-east-region/east-region-/na-na-twin-arches.html",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The arches overlook a deep gorge carved over time by tributaries that feed the South Fork of the Cumberland River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15,
